## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of advanced bit-twiddling for permutation of set bits. Evaluates ability to craft constant-time logic without loops and to reason about edge cases of the last permutation.

## Key Concepts

- Next higher permutation with fixed popcount
- Edge-case handling (max permutation -> 0)
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts; no loops or branches
- Efficient 64-bit handling and overflow behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = ripple & -ripple;
  uint64_t next = ripple | (((ones ^ ripple) >> 2) / smallest);
  return next;
}
```

## Examples

- `nextBitPermutation64(0b0011)` returns `0b0101` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b1100)` returns `0b10000` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b111000)` returns `0b1000011` (next permutation with 3 set bits)

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of cross-limb bit indexing and non-trivial bit-twiddling without branching. It introduces a different angle: packing every other bit across a 128-bit value into a 64-bit result.

## Key Concepts

- **Piece-wise bit extraction with masks:** Isolate target bits using bitwise AND with appropriate masks.
- **In-limb and cross-limb mapping:** Map even bits within each 64-bit limb, then combine results.
- **Parallel bit-pack technique:** Use the classic "compress" trick—mask, shift, and merge repeatedly to collapse bits.
- **Endianness and limb numbering:** The `lo` limb contains bits 0-63, and `hi` contains bits 64-127 (little-endian representation).

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint64_t packEvenBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;              // keep even bits
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 16)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 16)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;

  return (hi << 32) | lo;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to decompose a 128-bit operation into smaller parallel units and efficiently apply a well-known branchless counting technique.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount for 32-bit lanes
- 128-bit value decomposition into hi/lo components
- Branchless bit manipulation and final reduction

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount32(unsigned int x) {
    x = x - ((x>>1)&0x55555555);
    x = (x&0x33333333)+((x>>2)&0x33333333);
    x = (x+(x>>4))&0x0F0F0F0F;
    return (x*0x01010101)>>24;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount32(x.lo) + popcount32(x.lo >> 32) + 
           popcount32(x.hi) + popcount32(x.hi >> 32);
}
```

## Quick Tests
```c
assert(popcount128(0) == 0);
assert(popcount128(UINT128_MAX) == 128);
assert(popcount128(0xF0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0) == 32);
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply bit-dilation technique and Morton code basics, a common need in graphics or spatial indexing.

## Key Concepts

- Bit spreading (dilate) with masks
- Interleaving two bit streams into a 64-bit value
- No-loops approach with fixed masks

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t morton2D64(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  uint64_t dx = x;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

  uint64_t dy = y;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

  return dx | (dy << 1);
}
```

